# global-code-structure

> **Purpose:** Keep every file small, commented, DRY and easy to read.
>
> **Applies to:** Every code file in the repo (always on).

## File header
- Start every code file with a header comment: what the file is for, what it exports, who uses it.

```ts
/**
 * File: hash-url.ts
 * Purpose: Builds a stable SHA-256 key for a URL (used as page_cache primary key).
 * Exports: hashUrl
 * Used by: services/page-cache.ts, agent/nodes/fetch.ts
 */
```

## Comments
- Comment the "why" of non-obvious logic. Never comment every line.
- Add a one-line doc comment to every exported function.
- Delete comments that no longer match the code.

## Size
- Keep every file at 200 lines or fewer.
- 300 lines is the absolute maximum, for rare cases only.
- Above 200 lines, split the file by responsibility.
- Keep functions under about 40 lines, with one job each.

## DRY
- When code repeats a second time, extract it into a helper or util.
- Never copy-paste logic between files. Import it.
- Put a helper in the right place:
  - used by one app: that app's `utils/`
  - part of the shared contract (for example `slugify`): `@stamp/schema`
- Name helpers by what they do: `normalizeText`, `hashUrl`, `withTimeout`.

## Style
- Prefer plain functions and composition.
- No factories, class hierarchies or heavy design patterns.
- Prefer pure functions; keep I/O at the edges.
- No dead code and no commented-out code.
